Sign a `.bhive`, or remove its signatures (docs/bhive.md §13). A signature says who made the
archive's contents and that they haven't changed since; changing who can open it keeps it,
changing the contents drops it. Signing an archive that has no cell digests yet reads every
cell once.

Usage: beezip sign [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE>

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive

Options:
      --sign <SIGNER>
          Sign with these: your signing key by name, a certificate's thumbprint in your stores, or a
          certificate file with its key beside it

      --remove <N>
          Remove these signatures, by their place in the list

      --remove-all[=<REMOVE_ALL>]
          Remove every signature
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The password or recovery key, for an encrypted archive none of your keys opens

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -i, --identity <FILE>
          A key file that opens encrypted .bhive archives (age identities, PEM and PKCS #8 keys);
          repeatable

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
